From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 15:05:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: do not cache a video frame for a hardware client

Closing a hardware-rendered game segfaulted Kodi on the way out, in the
savestate the player writes as it closes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp	2026-09-07 15:01:46.804667953 +1200
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp	2026-09-07 15:01:46.816494459 +1200
@@ -1023,6 +1023,20 @@
 
 void CRPRenderManager::CacheVideoFrame(const std::string& savestatePath)
 {
+  // Nothing to cache for a hardware-rendering client, and trying crashes.
+  //
+  // The frame lives in a framebuffer object on the GPU, not in a buffer with
+  // pixels a savestate thumbnail could be made from without a readback. The
+  // buffers here are also the pool's, and by the time CloseFile() asks for a
+  // savestate they have been released - so this walked freed memory and
+  // segfaulted on the way out of every hardware-rendered game:
+  //
+  //   CRPRenderManager::CacheVideoFrame
+  //   CReversiblePlayback::CreateSavestate
+  //   CRetroPlayer::CloseFile
+  if (!m_hwBufferPools.empty())
+    return;
+
   std::unique_lock lock(m_bufferMutex);
 
   // Get the render buffers for this savestate path
